// Shape: same-generation with negation.
//
// Provenance: the "same generation" (SG) recursive query is a canonical
// Datalog benchmark -- Ullman, "Principles of Database and Knowledge-Base
// Systems", Vol I (1988), section on recursive query evaluation; the
// exact formulation used here (sg(x,x) base case, parent-pair recursive
// case) is the standard textbook one, unchanged across the literature.
// The negation extension is motivated by OpenRuleBench's "negation"
// category (Liang, Fodor, Wan, Kifer, "OpenRuleBench: An Analysis of the
// Performance of Rule Engines", WWW 2009), test name "same_generation" --
// OpenRuleBench's own source file could not be obtained
// (docs/reports/openrulebench-preregistration.md, 2026-08-21) so this is
// a reconstruction from the well-known problem definition, NOT a copy of
// OpenRuleBench's exact file. Cite the shape's provenance (Ullman +
// OpenRuleBench's category), not this file, as OpenRuleBench's own.
//
// Fixture: harness/fixtures_lib.py's gen_family_tree (parameters: seed,
// depth, branching). Root is always node 0.

.decl parent(child:number, par:number)
.input parent

.decl person(x:number)
.input person

.decl sg(x:number, y:number)
sg(x,x) :- person(x).
sg(x,y) :- parent(x,xp), parent(y,yp), sg(xp,yp), x != y.

.decl notsg(x:number, y:number)
notsg(x,y) :- person(x), person(y), !sg(x,y).

.decl q_notsg(y:number)
.output q_notsg
q_notsg(y) :- notsg(0,y).
